{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbFVjBS9YyexvbQk2UtU", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Yes, but only with `--dry-run`. A project memory note warns that in staging, `EXPORT_BUCKET` points at the **production** bucket, so a real staging run could write to prod. I couldn't check this from the repo because the staging environment config isn't here. Treat the warning as true unless you've confirmed otherwise.\n\n```bash\npython scripts/export.py --env staging --dry-run\n```\n\nAlso note:\n\n- **The script doesn't upload anything yet.** Right now `scripts/export.py` only prints `exporting <env> to <bucket> (dry_run=...)` (line 11). Running it today is harmless and just shows you which bucket it resolved.\n- **`--env` doesn't pick the bucket.** The bucket comes only from the `EXPORT_BUCKET` environment variable, and defaults to `ledgerline-exports-dev` when it's unset (line 10). Passing `--env staging` won't select a staging bucket. In a staging shell it will use whatever `EXPORT_BUCKET` is set to, which is reportedly the prod bucket. Check that line of output before trusting a run.\n- **Safer option:** you can set the variable yourself for the test, e.g. `EXPORT_BUCKET=ledgerline-exports-dev python scripts/export.py --env staging --dry-run`.\n\nIf the script gets real upload code, it would be worth having it refuse to write when `--env` isn't `prod` but the bucket looks like a prod bucket."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 974, "cache_read_input_tokens": 18680, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 974}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "8d9927ab-4ea2-48e6-a78e